/// The regolith albedo model: a single parametric reflectance spectrum fitted
/// to a broad corpus of soils, rocks, and weathered minerals, broad enough to
/// also cover made ground like asphalt, concrete, and brick. Tabulated
/// logit-space component curves are combined linearly per wavelength with
/// weights derived from eleven normalized [0, 1] parameters, and the logistic
/// link maps the result back to a reflectance, so every output is strictly
/// inside (0, 1) by construction. Every parameter is saturated to [0, 1] at
/// entry.
///
/// Starting points for 58 familiar materials ship as the `REGOLITH_PRESET_*`
/// constants at the end of this module: soils named by their WRB/FAO soil
/// group, rocks by lithology, and made ground. Each is an 11-component vector
/// in the parameter order below, to be expanded with call-site `inline`.
///
/// Every preset is one real measured spectrum at its own fitted coordinates,
/// and reconstructs that measurement within 1.5 dE. Note the nonzero
/// `moisture` in nominally dry presets: air-dried laboratory spectra carry
/// residual hygroscopic moisture, which this model keeps on its `moisture`
/// axis instead of baking it into the material.
#smdl

using ::math import *;

// The logistic link, mapping a logit-space value back to (0, 1). The rails
// are float32 housekeeping, not a modeling choice: past |z| ~ 17 the true
// value would round to exactly 1.0, handing an infinity to any caller that
// takes -log(1 - R). The extrapolated corners of the parameter box do get
// there, so the rails are what keep the strict (0, 1) guarantee.
@(pure macro)
auto link(const auto z) = #min(#max(1.0 / (1.0 + #exp(-z)), 1.0e-7), 0.9999999);

/// The minimum wavelength of the regolith albedo table in nanometers.
export const float REGOLITH_MIN_WAVELENGTH = 400.0;
/// The maximum wavelength of the regolith albedo table in nanometers.
export const float REGOLITH_MAX_WAVELENGTH = 2500.0;
/// The number of entries in the regolith albedo table.
export const int REGOLITH_TABLE_SIZE = 138;

// smdl format off
